WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.250
Film Courage: One of your many books Ken is
WRITE TIME?

00:00:03.250 --> 00:00:07.629
And in the forward you say that the world
can be divided into two people, productive

00:00:07.629 --> 00:00:09.599
people and non-productive people.

00:00:09.599 --> 00:00:12.430
And you say that productive people have a
love affair with time.

00:00:12.430 --> 00:00:19.260
I’ve love to know what makes someone on
the right side of time and what make someone

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:21.590
where time in their enemy?

00:00:21.590 --> 00:00:27.730
Dr, Ken Atchity, Producer/Author: Well that’s
a very good question put in a very intelligent

00:00:27.730 --> 00:00:35.469
way that makes it hard to get a handle on
it because time is…time doesn’t really

00:00:35.469 --> 00:00:36.620
exist.

00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:41.199
Time is a human construct, we created time.

00:00:41.199 --> 00:00:45.290
Squirrels and chipmunks don’t have much
idea of time.

00:00:45.290 --> 00:00:50.739
They know that the sun rises and the sun goes
down and they know that it rains but they

00:00:50.739 --> 00:00:56.010
don’t think the way that we do and they
don’t keep track of their birthdays for

00:00:56.010 --> 00:00:58.070
example, only humans do that.

00:00:58.070 --> 00:01:02.839
And it’s unfortunate because you’re only
as old as you think you are.

00:01:02.839 --> 00:01:07.250
And that’s the way a squirrel looks at it
and nobody is arguing with the squirrel about

00:01:07.250 --> 00:01:12.260
it but humans know better.

00:01:12.260 --> 00:01:17.010
Some people look at time as the enemy and
some people look at it as a friend.

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:21.940
There is an old Spanish saying that is “There
is more time than life,” which I always

00:01:21.940 --> 00:01:26.060
thought was a wonderful way of looking at
it because that is what a productive person

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:28.810
would say “there is more time than life.”

00:01:28.810 --> 00:01:40.100
And another Spanish or Italian saying says
that “Life is short, but wide.”

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:43.630
And that’s another way of productively looking
at it.

00:01:43.630 --> 00:01:46.960
Like people say “How can you do as much
stuff as you do?”

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:48.810
Well that’s because that’s what I do.

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:50.950
I don’t do anything else.

00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:55.620
And I used to give classes on time management
and do a lot of studies on it, in fact WRITE

00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:58.730
TIME is filled with time management theories.

00:01:58.730 --> 00:02:04.050
And one of the things I noticed about people
was they had no idea where there time went.

00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:07.330
And they go “I don’t know where you find
all the time.”

00:02:07.330 --> 00:02:09.759
And I would say “I don’t know where you
lose it.”

00:02:09.759 --> 00:02:12.870
I mean we all have the same amount of time
and they go “How much time do we have by

00:02:12.870 --> 00:02:13.870
the way?

00:02:13.870 --> 00:02:15.049
How much time is in a week?”

00:02:15.049 --> 00:02:20.049
And 2 out of 10 people can ask the question
right off the top of their heads because they’ve

00:02:20.049 --> 00:02:27.939
never really multiplied 25 by 7 and realized
exactly how many hours there are in a week.

00:02:27.939 --> 00:02:31.129
Everybody has the same amount of time.

00:02:31.129 --> 00:02:36.310
So what I would do in a time management class
at UCLA or elsewhere is I would say let’s

00:02:36.310 --> 00:02:38.100
chart your time this week.

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:44.239
I just want you to make a chart of what you
do with your time and let’s come in and

00:02:44.239 --> 00:02:46.629
talk about it next week when we come back
together.

00:02:46.629 --> 00:02:52.299
And they would come back in and that was before
I asked them how many hours there were in

00:02:52.299 --> 00:02:56.439
a week I would wait for the third week to
ask that question.

00:02:56.439 --> 00:03:02.540
And some people would come in with 98 hour
weeks and some people would come in with 62

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:09.580
hour weeks and nobody seem to agree in general
how many hours there were in a week because

00:03:09.580 --> 00:03:13.430
the hours they gave me didn’t add up, they
didn’t make sense.

00:03:13.430 --> 00:03:16.450
They’d say “I sleep six hours a day.”

00:03:16.450 --> 00:03:23.000
But it turned out in the third week of analysis
that instead of 6 hours a day they were actually

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:24.889
sleeping 10 hours.

00:03:24.889 --> 00:03:28.379
They just were telling themselves they were
sleeping 6 hours a day.

00:03:28.379 --> 00:03:31.280
How much time do you spend talking on the
telephone?

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:36.599
Most people thought they maybe spent 15 minutes
a day, when in fact it might be an hour a

00:03:36.599 --> 00:03:38.810
day.

00:03:38.810 --> 00:03:41.069
And watching television (of course).

00:03:41.069 --> 00:03:45.180
Some people said they were only watching an
hour a day when they were actually watching

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:47.450
three hours a day.

00:03:47.450 --> 00:03:54.040
But a productive person knows exactly how
long it takes to do something.

00:03:54.040 --> 00:03:58.870
Like when I write a screenplay or a book,
I can tell you how many hours it takes to

00:03:58.870 --> 00:04:03.090
do it and so I know that I can get it done
in a certain amount of time.

00:04:03.090 --> 00:04:06.809
Agatha Christie apparently wrote as many as
10 books a year.

00:04:06.809 --> 00:04:12.010
She had to use four or five pen names because
she just kept writing.

00:04:12.010 --> 00:04:19.190
When you think about it writing is a function
of how fast you type.

00:04:19.190 --> 00:04:24.770
Because I always say (in my writing book including
that one) if you’re making a rule not to

00:04:24.770 --> 00:04:30.960
sit down to write if you don’t know what
you’re going to write then you’ll never

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:33.590
waste any time and you’ll never have writer’s
block.

00:04:33.590 --> 00:04:37.539
So simply don’t sit down until you know
what you’re going to write.

00:04:37.539 --> 00:04:40.770
It’s just a matter of how fast can you type.

00:04:40.770 --> 00:04:47.039
So it’s better to be walking along the beach
thinking about the structure of your story

00:04:47.039 --> 00:04:51.509
then it is to be wasting a lot of time sitting
in front of the computer typing stuff and

00:04:51.509 --> 00:04:53.620
throwing it away and all that stuff.

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:55.020
Just figure it all out in your head.

00:04:55.020 --> 00:04:56.910
“Well what if I forget it?”

00:04:56.910 --> 00:04:57.910
Well guess what?

00:04:57.910 --> 00:04:59.599
If you forget it that’s probably good.

00:04:59.599 --> 00:05:02.330
You are forgetting forgettable things?

00:05:02.330 --> 00:05:05.610
You won’t forget it when it starts getting
really good.

00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:09.821
Because then it will do what Faulkner said,
it will start haunting you and you won’t

00:05:09.821 --> 00:05:13.139
be able to forget it and then you’ll just
write it down.

00:05:13.139 --> 00:05:17.580
William Saroyan was asked once how long it
took him to write the Human Comedy because

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:24.520
somebody had told the journalist it had took
him three days and he said “No, it took

00:05:24.520 --> 00:05:25.990
me all my life to write it.

00:05:25.990 --> 00:05:29.930
It just took me a few days to type it out.”

00:05:29.930 --> 00:05:33.590
So if you’re productive you’ve already
figured out that there are certain things

00:05:33.590 --> 00:05:37.879
that are completely unproductive such as sitting
in front of a blank screen trying to figure

00:05:37.879 --> 00:05:45.200
out what to put down next and other ways to
do things that make you productive.

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:48.080
And productive people don’t waste their
time.

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:53.449
As I said when it comes to waiting, you don’t
wait, you just do something else.

00:05:53.449 --> 00:05:59.770
You what I call “rotate” from one thing
to another so you still have new energy constantly

00:05:59.770 --> 00:06:01.870
because you’re switching to new activities.

00:06:01.870 --> 00:06:05.069
And when you switch to the new activity you
have new energy just because of that.

00:06:05.069 --> 00:06:09.530
But you’re also pulling energy from the
previous activity, it’s kind of pulling

00:06:09.530 --> 00:06:11.970
you back and wanting you to do more on it.

00:06:11.970 --> 00:06:17.280
But that’s good instead of listening to
it and going back and doing more on the previous

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:18.280
activity.

00:06:18.280 --> 00:06:22.940
It’s better to have that kind of little
anxiety going on there because then the next

00:06:22.940 --> 00:06:29.310
time that activity gets a chance at your time
it will be ready and it will be more productive

00:06:29.310 --> 00:06:31.659
during that time compartment.

00:06:31.659 --> 00:06:37.729
So I think that’s the difference between
productive and unproductive people have never

00:06:37.729 --> 00:06:43.300
figured out how to use time, they don’t
even know how to measure time.

00:06:43.300 --> 00:06:46.100
And they confuse things.

00:06:46.100 --> 00:06:50.569
There are two function in life or two entities
that we deal with.

00:06:50.569 --> 00:06:53.110
One is time and the other is work.

00:06:53.110 --> 00:07:00.289
And one of them is eternal and timeless and
endless and the other one is not.

00:07:00.289 --> 00:07:01.699
But people get it wrong.

00:07:01.699 --> 00:07:09.470
The one that is timeless and endless and eternal
is work not time unless you’re God, you

00:07:09.470 --> 00:07:10.470
know?

00:07:10.470 --> 00:07:11.969
But if you’re not God, then guess what?

00:07:11.969 --> 00:07:16.539
You have a limited amount of time and the
only problem is you don’t know what the

00:07:16.539 --> 00:07:20.039
limit is but that doesn’t matter because
you just have to operate anyway.

00:07:20.039 --> 00:07:28.080
But what is infinite is work because good
work produces more work and so does bad work.

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:33.729
So no matter what kind of work you’re doing
it keeps going and you cannot manage it therefore

00:07:33.729 --> 00:07:39.900
because it’s a given that you can’t manage
an infinite thing but you can manage something

00:07:39.900 --> 00:07:43.250
that is finite and that’s time.

00:07:43.250 --> 00:07:45.650
So managing time is what we have to do.

00:07:45.650 --> 00:07:51.210
So let’s say that if you’re writing a
book and you know that you type 7 pages an

00:07:51.210 --> 00:07:58.349
hour at least then you give yourself one 1
hour every day to write your book.

00:07:58.349 --> 00:08:02.491
Well at the end of 100 days you’ve got how
many pages?

00:08:02.491 --> 00:08:06.270
700 pages right?

00:08:06.270 --> 00:08:09.600
So it’s not complicated to figure it out.

00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:13.800
But you’ve got to manage the right thing,
manage your time.

00:08:13.800 --> 00:08:21.139
Because the work will happen only if you give
it time to attend to it and what happens to

00:08:21.139 --> 00:08:26.330
the people who procrastinate because they
think they’re trying to manager the work

00:08:26.330 --> 00:08:29.270
and they don’t know that they can’t manage
the work.

00:08:29.270 --> 00:08:34.020
Like I’m going to get this work done if
it takes me all summer and then nothing happens

00:08:34.020 --> 00:08:35.529
(they don’t do it).

00:08:35.529 --> 00:08:36.660
That isn’t what you should do.

00:08:36.660 --> 00:08:41.930
You should say “I’m going to work from
seven to eight every morning without fail

00:08:41.930 --> 00:08:47.800
for five days a week rather than seven days
a week” because your brain revolts when

00:08:47.800 --> 00:08:52.340
you make it stop something that it’s actually
enjoying.

00:08:52.340 --> 00:08:58.560
So if you make it stop after the fifth day
it’s very upset and it spends the whole

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:03.620
weekend thinking about the project and it’s
really raring to go on Monday when you start

00:09:03.620 --> 00:09:04.620
again.

00:09:04.620 --> 00:09:08.589
Whereas if you keep it going, it will get
worn out and it will get bored eventually

00:09:08.589 --> 00:09:12.370
because that’s what brains do.

00:09:12.370 --> 00:09:16.880
So there are two kinds of productive people.

00:09:16.880 --> 00:09:19.130
The unproductive ones, let’s now talk about.

00:09:19.130 --> 00:09:24.149
I mean they have their own thing going and
I hope they are enjoying life.

00:09:24.149 --> 00:09:28.190
But productive people are divided into two
kinds and those are the happy ones and the

00:09:28.190 --> 00:09:29.649
unhappy ones.

00:09:29.649 --> 00:09:35.570
The unhappy ones are the ones that never figured
out the psychology of creativity and so they

00:09:35.570 --> 00:09:40.310
are constantly surprised by it and upset by
it.

00:09:40.310 --> 00:09:46.480
And that’s why you have Virginia Wolf and
Hemingway and Sylvia Plath offing themselves

00:09:46.480 --> 00:09:49.160
at the end because they’ve never figured
it out.

00:09:49.160 --> 00:09:53.550
They’ve never figured out at the end of
a project they are going to get depressed

00:09:53.550 --> 00:09:59.480
and they are going to go into postpartum depression
that they may never come out of.

00:09:59.480 --> 00:10:04.449
But if you’re on the other side of tings
(the happy productive person) you’ve figured

00:10:04.449 --> 00:10:05.550
that out all already.

00:10:05.550 --> 00:10:06.870
So what do you do?

00:10:06.870 --> 00:10:12.170
Before you end a project, you end another
project and then you can’t wait to get into

00:10:12.170 --> 00:10:17.260
the new project so you don’t mind finishing
the first project so you’ve eliminated postpartum

00:10:17.260 --> 00:10:22.920
depression and that’s simply because you’ve
figured out how your creative mind works which

00:10:22.920 --> 00:10:31.490
is what WRITER’S TIME is all about and that’s
what I mean by happy productive versus unhappy

00:10:31.490 --> 00:10:32.490
productive people.

00:10:32.490 --> 00:10:36.380
You don’t have to be miserable and suicidal
to be a writer.

00:10:36.380 --> 00:10:42.370
You can be perfectly happy by knowing your
system and not letting it do it to you.

